{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful figure in the 12th century Mediterranean world. He ruled as Count of Tripoli from 1146 to 1193 a period marked by both territorial expansion and military conflict. His reign saw the consolidation of Tripoli's power and influence in the Levant a region strategically important for trade and control.\n\n*Early Life and Ascendancy* Raymond's lineage traced back to the Counts of Tripoli a noble family who had held the title for generations. He inherited the countship upon the death of his father Raymond XII. Initially Raymond focused on consolidating his authority within Tripoli's territory. He dealt with local rivalries and strengthened the county's defenses. He also engaged in diplomatic efforts seeking alliances and securing favorable terms with neighboring powers.  His early years were characterized by shrewd political maneuvering and a pragmatic approach to governance. He fostered economic growth and improved the lives of his subjects.\n\nRaymond III's reign was significantly shaped by his military campaigns. He expanded Tripoli's territory through a series of successful expeditions. These campaigns targeted Muslim territories along the Mediterranean coast. He gained control of key coastal towns and established a strong presence in the region.  He also engaged in conflicts with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and other Crusader states. His military successes enhanced Tripoli's prestige and solidified its position as a major power in the Levant.  He was known for his military acumen and his ability to effectively command his troops. \n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*  Raymond III was a skilled diplomat who sought to maintain a delicate balance of power in the region. He forged alliances with various powers including the Kingdom of Jerusalem the Byzantine Empire and other Crusader states. These alliances were essential for Tripoli's security and for its ability to project its power. He engaged in complex negotiations seeking to secure favorable trade agreements and to protect Tripoli's interests. He often played a mediating role in disputes between other powers. His diplomatic efforts were often aimed at preventing large-scale conflicts and maintaining stability in the Mediterranean.  He was a key player in shaping the political landscape of the Levant during the 12th century.\n\n*Legacy and Decline* Raymond III's reign was a period of prosperity and expansion for Tripoli. He left behind a strong and well-defended county with a thriving economy. However his reign also saw the gradual decline of Tripoli's power in the later years of his rule. The rise of new powers and the increasing instability of the region challenged Tripoli's position.  He died in 1193 leaving behind a legacy of military prowess and diplomatic skill. His reign is remembered as a golden age for Tripoli a time when the county reached its peak of power and influence.  The challenges of the late 12th century ultimately proved too great for Tripoli to overcome and the county gradually lost its prominence in the Mediterranean.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1805190145969391}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -5.686121585313231e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.09215376526117325}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.03319740295410156}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5858225226402283}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -1.4153985977172852}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.022406859323382378}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0035339067690074444}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.6943659782409668}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0013236580416560173}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": 